<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- RadarControls — the GLOBAL time module, and only that. Laid out entirely with Grids (no StackPanels):

       [———————————— scrubber ————————————]
                              10:42 PM  2 min ago
       ◀ ▶ ▶  3 / 10  │       KTLX      VCP 212 · precip · SAILS ×2
                              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
                              the site readout is a 2x2 BLOCK: time over site,
                              age over scan. Stacking it (it used to be one long
                              line) is what keeps this half narrow.

     THE ORGANIZING RULE: the bar holds what does NOT repeat per pane. One site, one camera, one time
     cursor, shared by every pane — which is why the scrubber does not multiply.

     ⚠️ THE PANE CHIPS AND THE TILT COMBO ARE GONE FROM HERE. This control used to be two clusters,
     [pane chips] │ [time module], where a chip was a 160px combined product-selector-and-colour-ramp, one
     per pane, growing to a 2x2 in quad. Both halves of that — the chips and the one tilt combo in the
     control row — moved into the per-pane NOTCH at the top of each map pane (Composites/PaneNotchContent),
     where a pane's legend sits on the field it describes. What is left is global, so there is no per-pane
     cluster, no divider between clusters, and no ChipWidth.

     ⚠️ AND SO THE WIDTH BUDGET IS GONE. The old note here said every pixel added on this side ate into
     the room the centred temporal keys need, and named ChipWidth as the knob to reach for first. The chips
     were the growth risk — 160px per pane, doubled in quad — and they are not here any more. This cluster
     is now a fixed size that does not track the pane layout at all.

     SCRUBBER WIDTH is still deliberately NOT the full bar: the module is auto-width, so the scrubber
     stretches to exactly the control row beneath it and stops at the end of the site readout. Every
     variable-length readout in that row (frame count, time, ICAO, age, scan) keeps a RESERVED fixed width,
     so the module's width — and therefore the scrubber's — is a constant. Without that the scrubber would
     resize (and reflow all its cells) every time the age ticked over. That rule survived the readout being
     stacked into a block — the block's two COLUMNS carry the reserved widths now — and it survived the
     chips leaving, which changed nothing about it.

     The global controls that flank this (temporal keys at the bar's centre, window buttons at the right)
     are composed by MainWindow; site-marker visibility and Inspect live in the Settings window's Radar
     tab. All bound to one RadarViewModel. -->
<UserControl x:Class="Anvil.Controls.Composites.RadarControls"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="using:Anvil.ViewModels"
             xmlns:layout="using:Anvil.Layout"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <x:Double x:Key="ReadoutFontSize">13</x:Double>
        <!-- The one vertical rule left, between the frame counter and the site block. (It was 34 tall
             when a chip cluster sat beside it; the sole consumer overrode it to 20, so that is the value now.) -->
        <Style x:Key="BarDividerStyle" TargetType="Border">
            <Setter Property="Width" Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="Height" Value="20"/>
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.4"/>
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource TextFillColorTertiaryBrush}"/>
        </Style>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!-- ONE cluster now, left-aligned: MainWindow centres the temporal keys and right-aligns the window
         buttons in the same bar row, and the gaps between the three are empty bar by design.

         ⚠️ It used to be TWO clusters — [pane chips] │ [time module] — and the outer grid, the divider and
         the ChipWidth resource all existed to hold them apart. The chips (and the tilt combo that sat in
         the control row below) moved into the pane notches, so this control IS the time module and needs
         no column structure at all. Everything left here is global; a per-pane control belongs in
         Composites/PaneNotchContent.

         The grid is auto-width: row 1 is all fixed widths, so it sets the module's width, and row 0's
         scrubber stretches to match it exactly. -->
    <Grid HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Center" RowSpacing="8">
        <!-- ⚠️ THE Auto COLUMN IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness. A Grid with no ColumnDefinitions gets one
             implicit STAR column, and a star column measures its children with the width AVAILABLE — which
             here is half the bar. EqualCellsPanel then reports that whole width as the scrubber's desired
             size and the scrubber stretches most of the way to the centred temporal keys. An Auto column
             measures with INFINITY, EqualCellsPanel returns 0, and row 1 is left to set the module width,
             which is the rule the header note describes. The old outer cluster grid supplied this by
             accident (the module sat in one of its Auto columns); losing it when the chips left is what
             broke the scrubber. -->
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- segmented scrubber -->
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- transport · frame · site readout -->
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- Segmented scrubber: one cell per frame (ViewModel.Segments), each lighting up as its frame
             decodes — the load indicator for the whole loop. The playhead is repositioned in
             code-behind over the current segment; pointer press/drag seeks. It measures to zero width
             on its own (EqualCellsPanel returns 0 under infinite width), which is what lets the
             control row below define the module width. -->
        <Grid x:Name="ScrubberHost"
              Grid.Row="0"
              Height="16"
              Background="Transparent"
              IsHitTestVisible="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsTransportEnabled, Mode=OneWay}"
              Opacity="{x:Bind ScrubberOpacity(ViewModel.IsTransportEnabled), Mode=OneWay}"
              SizeChanged="OnScrubberSizeChanged"
              PointerPressed="OnScrubberPointerPressed"
              PointerMoved="OnScrubberPointerMoved"
              PointerReleased="OnScrubberPointerReleased"
              PointerCaptureLost="OnScrubberPointerReleased"
              PointerCanceled="OnScrubberPointerReleased">
            <!-- Always-present track so the scrubber occupies the same footprint whether or not a loop
                 is loaded (the cells render on top as frames decode). -->
            <Border Height="6"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    CornerRadius="3"
                    Background="{ThemeResource ControlFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{x:Bind ViewModel.Segments, Mode=OneWay}"
                          IsHitTestVisible="False"
                          VerticalAlignment="Center"
                          Height="8">
                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <layout:EqualCellsPanel/>
                    </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:RadarFrameSegment">
                        <Border Margin="1,0"
                                CornerRadius="2"
                                Background="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}"
                                Opacity="{x:Bind ReadyOpacity, Mode=OneWay}"/>
                    </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
            <Border x:Name="Playhead"
                    Width="3"
                    Height="16"
                    CornerRadius="1.5"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    Background="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False">
                <Border.RenderTransform>
                    <TranslateTransform x:Name="PlayheadTransform"/>
                </Border.RenderTransform>
            </Border>
        </Grid>

        <!-- Control row. EVERY text column has a reserved fixed width (see the header note) so the
             module — and the scrubber above it — never changes width as the readouts update. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="1" ColumnSpacing="10">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- prev -->
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- play/stop -->
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- next -->
                <ColumnDefinition Width="52"/>   <!-- frame N / M -->
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- divider -->
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- site readout, stacked 2x2 -->
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <Button Grid.Column="0"
                    Style="{StaticResource TransportButtonStyle}"
                    Width="30"
                    Height="30"
                    Click="OnPrevClick"
                    IsEnabled="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsTransportEnabled, Mode=OneWay}"
                    ToolTipService.ToolTip="Previous frame">
                <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE892;" FontSize="13"/>
            </Button>
            <Button Grid.Column="1"
                    Style="{StaticResource TransportButtonStyle}"
                    Width="38"
                    Height="38"
                    Click="OnPlayStopClick"
                    IsEnabled="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsTransportEnabled, Mode=OneWay}"
                    ToolTipService.ToolTip="Play / stop (stop returns to the newest frame)">
                <FontIcon Glyph="{x:Bind PlayStopGlyph(ViewModel.IsPlaying), Mode=OneWay}" FontSize="17"/>
            </Button>
            <Button Grid.Column="2"
                    Style="{StaticResource TransportButtonStyle}"
                    Width="30"
                    Height="30"
                    Click="OnNextClick"
                    IsEnabled="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsTransportEnabled, Mode=OneWay}"
                    ToolTipService.ToolTip="Next frame">
                <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE893;" FontSize="13"/>
            </Button>

            <!-- Frame N/M (current / total). -->
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="3"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       Text="{x:Bind FrameCountText(ViewModel.CurrentFrameIndex, ViewModel.MaxFrameIndex), Mode=OneWay}"
                       FontSize="{StaticResource ReadoutFontSize}"
                       Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorTertiaryBrush}"
                       ToolTipService.ToolTip="Which frame of the loop is on screen"/>
            <Border Grid.Column="4" Style="{StaticResource BarDividerStyle}"/>

            <!-- ===== SITE READOUT — a 2x2 block, not a line =====
                   10:42 PM   2 min ago            row 0: WHEN (time of the frame on screen,
                   KTLX       VCP 212 · precip …   then how old the newest one is)
                                                   row 1: WHERE (site, then how it's scanning)

                 It was one long line; stacking it halves the readout's footprint, which is the
                 whole point — that width came straight off the time module (and so off the
                 scrubber), buying back room in the bar for the bigger temporal pills at centre.
                 The pairing also reads better than the old order did: the two TIME facts share a
                 row, the two SITE facts share the row under it, and each column's two values line
                 up on one left edge.

                 ⚠️ Both columns keep a RESERVED FIXED WIDTH for the same reason the old row did —
                 the module is auto-width, so any readout free to resize (the age ticking over, a
                 VCP change) would resize the scrubber above and reflow every one of its cells. -->
            <Grid Grid.Column="5"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  RowSpacing="2"
                  ColumnSpacing="10">
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- frame time · age -->
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- site ICAO · scan strategy -->
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="64"/>  <!-- frame time / ICAO -->
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="200"/> <!-- age / scan strategy -->
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <!-- The DISPLAYED frame's time (the one you scrubbed to), not the newest. -->
                <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.CurrentFrameTimeText, Mode=OneWay}"
                           FontSize="{StaticResource ReadoutFontSize}"
                           ToolTipService.ToolTip="When the frame on screen was scanned"/>
                <!-- Age of the freshest frame, color-ramped fresh(green) to stale(red) by AgeBrush so
                     freshness reads at a glance. Sits beside the frame time because the two together
                     answer one question: how current is what I'm looking at. -->
                <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.RadarAgeText, Mode=OneWay}"
                           FontSize="{StaticResource ReadoutFontSize}"
                           Foreground="{x:Bind AgeBrush(ViewModel.RadarAgeMinutes), Mode=OneWay}"
                           ToolTipService.ToolTip="How long ago the newest available frame was scanned. Green = fresh, red = stale."/>

                <!-- The site. The location name is the ICAO's tooltip — the ICAO is the real label —
                     and the rendered elevation is the Tilt combo, not here. -->
                <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.RadarSiteId, Mode=OneWay}"
                           FontWeight="SemiBold"
                           FontSize="{StaticResource ReadoutFontSize}"
                           ToolTipService.ToolTip="{x:Bind ViewModel.RadarSiteName, Mode=OneWay}"/>
                <!-- Scan strategy for the whole volume; see RadarVcpText for why SAILS belongs here.
                     Beside the ICAO because it describes THAT radar's current behaviour. -->
                <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           Text="{x:Bind RadarVcpText(ViewModel.RadarModeText), Mode=OneWay}"
                           FontSize="{StaticResource ReadoutFontSize}"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"
                           ToolTipService.ToolTip="The radar's scan strategy for the whole volume. VCP = Volume Coverage Pattern, a number chosen by the operator: 'precip' patterns scan faster and more often for rain/storms; 'clear-air' patterns scan slower for better sensitivity in dry weather. SAILS/MRLE ×N means the radar squeezes in N extra scans of the lowest tilt per volume, giving fresher data near the ground during fast-changing weather."/>
            </Grid>
        </Grid>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
